\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces A square with two adjacent block-aligned edges is a temperature switch. (a)~Ground states of rectangles by edge type (thick: block-aligned): only two adjacent block-aligned edges give a unique ground state that melts. (b)--(d)~Exact liquidity of the corner switch ($L=48$, boundary model) at three source strengths $q=J/2mT$, with the frozen boundaries of the scaling solution (red). (e)~Liquid fraction versus $q$ for $L=32$, 48, and 64 (points) and the scaling solution (line). The sample freezes completely at $q=\ln (1+\sqrt  2)$ (dotted).}}{7}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:switch}{{4}{7}{A square with two adjacent block-aligned edges is a temperature switch. (a)~Ground states of rectangles by edge type (thick: block-aligned): only two adjacent block-aligned edges give a unique ground state that melts. (b)--(d)~Exact liquidity of the corner switch ($L=48$, boundary model) at three source strengths $q=J/2mT$, with the frozen boundaries of the scaling solution (red). (e)~Liquid fraction versus $q$ for $L=32$, 48, and 64 (points) and the scaling solution (line). The sample freezes completely at $q=\ln (1+\sqrt 2)$ (dotted)}{figure.4}{}}
